DESCRIPTION:North Carolina’s 1868 Constitution rebuilt the state after th
 e Civil War\, expanding political participation and redefining the relatio
 nship between government and citizens in the state’s first attempt at mu
 ltiracial democracy. This lecture examines how that document laid the grou
 ndwork for principles that continue to shape North Carolina constitutional
  law\, and what its lessons offer for constitutional debates still unresol
 ved 250 years after the state’s founding. \n\n \n\nSpeakers: Marcus Gads
 on\, a | Source: UNC Chapel Hill Events Calendar (university-wide) | Cost:
